builderBrief:
Build the Improved Acreage Router and make it the shared acreage input for LandOS comp selection and valuation.

THE ACCEPTANCE PROPERTY is 9490 Elk Lake Rd, Williamsburg, MI 49690. It is a real 60-acre subject in this repository's store (property card 73, deal card 83, Grand Traverse County MI, APN 13-116-015-01, lat 44.822439610896, lng -85.404821349666). LandOS already holds five real LandPortal sold land records for it: 40 ac / $400,000 / 2025-03-21, 85.32 ac / $1,100,000 / 2026-02-12, 40 ac / $400,000 / 2025-03-21 (a duplicate of the first), 40 ac / $375,000 / 2025-02-04, and 39.94 ac / $390,000 / 2025-04-04. That is roughly $9,375 to $12,893 per acre. Every one of them carries a stated sale date and NONE of them carries coordinates or an address. Today LandOS prices the subject from zero of them.

WHY, EXACTLY. There are three separate causes and you must fix all three.

1. THREE MODULES EACH INVENT THEIR OWN ACREAGE ASSUMPTION.
   `valuationAcreageBand` in `src/landos/comp-recency-window.ts` returns {min:40, max:160} for a 60-acre subject, so the 39.94-acre sale is thrown out by six hundredths of an acre.
   `acreageBand` in `src/landos/deal-intelligence-comps.ts` independently returns {lo:30, hi:150} for the same subject.
   `sameAcreageBand` in `src/landos/comparable-intelligence.ts` independently uses a label band plus a 0.5x-2x ratio.
   Comp selection and valuation therefore disagree about what the same subject's comparable set even is.

2. DISTANCE IS A HARD GATE INSTEAD OF A RANKING SIGNAL.
   `qualifySoldComparable` in `src/landos/comparable-intelligence.ts` rejects any row whose `distanceMiles` is null and any row past 10 miles. `MAX_COMP_SEARCH_RADIUS_MILES = 20` in `src/landos/comps-valuation.ts` is a hard boundary. The five 9490 LandPortal records have no coordinates at all, so all five are discarded for a distance nobody ever tried to compute.

3. A LANDPORTAL COMPARABLE WITH A STATED SALE DATE IS FILED AS UNKNOWN.
   `src/landos/hermes-landportal-import.ts` reads `comp.sale_date`, writes it to `saleOrListDate`, and builds a canonical key containing `sale:2026-02-12` — and then writes `priceKind: 'unknown'`. Downstream that becomes "Transaction status is not stated by the source" and the row is context only.

WHAT TO BUILD.

A. NEW MODULE `src/landos/acreage-router.ts`. Pure, deterministic, no I/O, no clock. Export exactly this surface, with these names:

   export type AcreageRegime = 'micro' | 'small' | 'mid' | 'large' | 'very_large';
   export interface AcreagePool { min: number; max: number; label: string }
   export interface AcreageRoute {
     subjectAcres: number;
     regime: AcreageRegime;
     regimeLabel: string;      // operator-facing
     pool: AcreagePool;        // broad window a row must sit in to participate at all
     preferred: AcreagePool;   // narrower window inside the pool where similarity peaks
     tightAcreageMatching: boolean;
     rankingEmphasis: string[];// what does the ranking once acreage stops discriminating
     rationale: string;        // >= 40 chars, operator-readable, says why the pool is this wide
   }
   export function routeAcreage(subjectAcres: number | null | undefined): AcreageRoute | null;
   export function inAcreagePool(acres: number | null | undefined, route: AcreageRoute | null): boolean;
   export function routedAcreageSimilarity(acres: number | null | undefined, route: AcreageRoute | null): number; // 0..1, 0 outside the pool

   Regimes, following the operator rule: 'micro' under 2 ac, 'small' 2 to under 10, 'mid' 10 to under 30, 'large' 30 through 100, 'very_large' above 100. `tightAcreageMatching` is true for micro, small and mid — dollars per acre changes materially between small size bands, so keep those tight. It is false for large and very_large.

   The 'large' pool must be genuinely broad: max/min at least 3, the floor at or below 0.35x the subject, the ceiling at or above 2.5x. For a 60-acre subject that admits 39.94, 40 and 85.32 acres. A 3-acre subject must still exclude a 40-acre sale, and its pool spread must be strictly tighter than the large-acreage spread. `routeAcreage` returns null for null, undefined, zero, negative or non-finite acreage — a route is never invented.

   `routedAcreageSimilarity` returns 1 at an exact match, falls off continuously, stays strictly positive everywhere inside the pool (a 40-acre sale must carry non-zero weight against a 60-acre subject; zero weight is the defect being repaired), and returns 0 outside the pool. 55 acres must outrank 40 acres for a 60-acre subject.

B. GEOGRAPHY, in the same module.

   export type GeographicTierId = 'local_10' | 'near_20' | 'regional_35' | 'extended_50' | 'county_market' | 'distance_unresolved';
   export interface GeographicTier {
     id: GeographicTierId; label: string; maxMiles: number | null;
     expanded: boolean; retained: boolean; weightMultiplier: number; rationale: string;
   }
   export const GEOGRAPHIC_TIERS: readonly GeographicTier[];
   export function resolveGeographicTier(distanceMiles: number | null | undefined): GeographicTier;
   export function compDistanceMiles(subject: { lat?: number | null; lng?: number | null } | null | undefined, comp: { lat?: number | null; lng?: number | null } | null | undefined): number | null;
   export function describeGeographicExpansion(input: { route: AcreageRoute | null; tiersUsed: GeographicTierId[]; usableCount: number }): string;

   Start local and expand outward on purpose. `expanded` is false only for 'local_10'. `retained` is true on EVERY tier including 'distance_unresolved': nothing is ever discarded on distance alone. `weightMultiplier` is in (0,1], equals 1 at 'local_10', and is non-increasing as distance grows. `resolveGeographicTier(null)` returns the 'distance_unresolved' tier, whose label and rationale say plainly that the location could not be resolved, and whose multiplier is below 1. `compDistanceMiles` is straight-line (haversine) miles and returns null whenever either side lacks finite coordinates — a distance is never invented. `describeGeographicExpansion` returns at least 40 characters, mentions miles, and names the label of every tier in `tiersUsed`.

C. STRUCTURED OUTPUT FOR A LATER STRATEGY AGENT — build the context, do NOT build Strategy.

   export interface AcreageMarketContext {
     route: AcreageRoute;
     subjectAcres: number;
     usableCount: number;
     pricePerAcre: { low: number | null; mid: number | null; high: number | null } | null;
     tiersUsed: GeographicTierId[];
     expansionExplanation: string;
   }
   export function buildAcreageMarketContext(input: { route: AcreageRoute | null; comps: Array<{ acres: number | null; pricePerAcre: number | null; distanceMiles: number | null }> }): AcreageMarketContext | null;

   This is the shared acreage and market context a later whole-versus-subdivision comparison will read. Add nothing else for Strategy.

D. MAKE THE ROUTER THE SINGLE AUTHORITY. Each of `src/landos/comp-recency-window.ts`, `src/landos/deal-intelligence-comps.ts`, `src/landos/comparable-intelligence.ts` and `src/landos/comps-valuation.ts` must import from `./acreage-router.js` and delegate to it. Do not copy the numbers.
   - `valuationAcreageBand(subjectAcres)` returns the router pool (same min and max), still null when there is no subject acreage.
   - `acreageBand(subjectAcres)` in deal-intelligence-comps returns { lo: pool.min, hi: pool.max }, still null when there is no subject acreage.
   - `sameAcreageBand` in comparable-intelligence resolves through `inAcreagePool`.
   Keep the existing exported names and shapes; only what they decide changes.

E. DISTANCE BECOMES A COMPUTED, VISIBLE RANKING SIGNAL, NOT A GATE.
   - Add optional `lat` and `lng` to `CompSelectionSubject` in deal-intelligence-comps.
   - In `selectWorkingComps`, when a row has no `distanceMiles` but both the subject and the row carry coordinates, compute it with `compDistanceMiles` and carry it onto the snapshot comp so the operator sees it.
   - A row is NEVER dropped for a null distance or for being past 20 miles. Resolve its tier instead, apply the tier's weight multiplier in `comparabilityScore`, and say the distance in the comp's operator text. A comp beyond the local radius must mention miles in its `whyUseful`, `similarities` or `differences`.
   - `qualifySoldComparable` in comparable-intelligence must stop rejecting a row for `distanceMiles == null` or for exceeding 10 miles; rank it down through the tier instead.
   - Remove the hard 20-mile discard in comps-valuation. Retain and rank past-20-mile evidence with its tier stated. `INITIAL_COMP_RADIUS_MILES` and `MAX_COMP_SEARCH_RADIUS_MILES` may stay as exported constants if other code reads them, but they must no longer delete evidence.
   - Add to `CompWorkingSet` in deal-intelligence-comps: `acreageRouting: AcreageRoute | null`, `geographicExpansion: string`, `acreageMarketContext: AcreageMarketContext | null`, populated by `selectWorkingComps`.

F. A HOUSE IS NOT VACANT-LAND COMPETITION.
   - Export from `src/landos/comps-valuation.ts`:
     export function detectImprovedProperty(input: { propertyClass?: string | null; classification?: string | null; notes?: string | null; addressDesc?: string | null; descriptionText?: string | null; buildingSqft?: number | null }): { improved: boolean; evidence: string | null };
     It returns improved:true for a residential property class, for building square footage of about 1,000 or more, and for text stating bedrooms, bathrooms, a home, a house, a residence or a dwelling; it returns improved:false for vacant-land text, for a land property class, and for an empty input. When improved is true, `evidence` names the signal that produced it. Use it inside `classifyPersistedComp` so an improved listing lands in `improved_context`, never in `active_competition`.
   - In `selectWorkingComps`, a row may enter the `active` lane and be described as vacant-land competition only when it is affirmatively vacant land. A row whose `landClass` is 'improved', or whose `landClass` is 'unknown' while it carries `homeSizeSqft`, `yearBuilt` or a residential `homeType`, must not appear there. Do not delete those rows: bucket them into `evidence` with a reason that names the structure signal, so the count and the reason stay visible.
   - Leave the `WORKSPACE_CATEGORY_LABELS` strings alone. What changes is which rows may enter the lane.

G. A LANDPORTAL COMPARABLE WITH A SOURCE-STATED SALE DATE IS A SOURCE-STATED SALE.
   - Export from `src/landos/deal-intelligence-comps.ts` (it is the pure module that already owns `CompStatusBasis`):
     export function landPortalSaleStatus(row: { source: string; dateIso: string | null; priceKind?: string | null }): { statusBasis: CompStatusBasis; provenance: string };
     A LandPortal row with a parseable stated sale date returns statusBasis 'closed_sale' and a provenance string naming LandPortal and the stated date. A LandPortal row with no stated date returns 'unconfirmed'. A row from any other source returns 'unconfirmed' — this helper governs LandPortal only and never changes how Zillow or Redfin rows are read.
   - `src/landos/hermes-landportal-import.ts` must use it to decide the persisted `priceKind` instead of hardcoding `'unknown'`, and must keep the provenance in the row's notes. Change nothing else about the import.
   - This reads what the source published. Do not invent a sale, a date, or a status anywhere.

RULES.
   - LandPortal comps are retained and RANKED with everything else. They must never win rank by being LandPortal.
   - Do not fabricate coordinates, distances, prices, dates or statuses.
   - Do not build the Strategy agent, Pre/Post Discovery Revaluation, dashboards, new providers, or a comp-collection redesign.
   - Change no file outside the allowed paths.
   - The existing comp suites assert the old bands. Update those assertions to the new routing. Do not delete tests, do not skip tests, and do not weaken an assertion to something trivially true. Add `src/landos/acreage-router.test.ts` covering the router, including the 60-acre subject with its real 39.94 and 85.32 acre records.
   - `node_modules` is shared into this worktree, so `npx vitest` and `npx tsc` both work here. If you are able to run shell commands, run `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx vitest run src/landos/acreage-router.test.ts src/landos/comp-recency-window.test.ts src/landos/comparable-intelligence.test.ts src/landos/deal-intelligence-comps.test.ts src/landos/comps-valuation.test.ts` before you finish. If you cannot run shell commands, be correspondingly careful: every type must line up by inspection.
